# Proof

**Yes**, such a continuous linear functional exists. We construct it in three steps.

## Step 1 — A convex open neighborhood separating $A$ from $B$

Since $B$ is closed and $A\cap B=\varnothing$, each $a\in A$ lies in the open set $X\setminus B$. By local convexity, there is a convex open neighborhood $V_a$ of $0$ with $a+V_a\subset X\setminus B$. The family $\{a+\tfrac12 V_a\}_{a\in A}$ is an open cover of the compact set $A$; extract a finite subcover indexed by $a_1,\dots,a_n$ and set
$$U'=\bigcap_{i=1}^n \tfrac12 V_{a_i},$$
a convex open neighborhood of $0$. Then
$$A+U'\subset\bigcup_{i=1}^n(a_i+V_{a_i})\subset X\setminus B,$$
so $(A+U')\cap B=\varnothing$. Setting $U=-U'$ (still convex, open, $0\in U$), we obtain
$$A\cap(B+U)=\varnothing. \tag{1}$$

## Step 2 — Separation via Hahn–Banach

The set $C:=(B+U)-A$ is convex (difference of the convex sets $B+U$ and $A$), open (since $B+U=\bigcup_{b\in B}(b+U)$ is a union of open translates), and satisfies $0\notin C$ by (1).

> **Lemma.** Let $E$ be a nonempty open convex set in a TVS and $x_0\notin E$. Then there exists a continuous linear functional $g$ with $\Re g(x)<\Re g(x_0)$ for all $x\in E$.
>
> *Proof of Lemma.* Pick $e_0\in E$ and set $F=E-e_0$ (open convex, $0\in F$). Let $p$ be the Minkowski functional of $F$; $p$ is continuous and sublinear with $F=\{x:p(x)<1\}$. Since $x_0-e_0\notin F$, we have $p(x_0-e_0)\ge 1$. Define a linear functional $\ell$ on $\operatorname{span}\{x_0-e_0\}$ by $\ell(x_0-e_0)=1$; one verifies $\ell\le p$ on this line (for $\lambda\ge0$: $\ell(\lambda(x_0-e_0))=\lambda\le\lambda\,p(x_0-e_0)=p(\lambda(x_0-e_0))$; for $\lambda<0$: $\ell(\lambda(x_0-e_0))=\lambda<0\le p(\lambda(x_0-e_0))$). By the Hahn–Banach theorem, extend to a real-linear $f\le p$ on all of $X$; $f$ is continuous because $p$ is. For $x\in E$: $x-e_0\in F$, so $f(x-e_0)\le p(x-e_0)<1=f(x_0-e_0)$, giving $f(x)<f(x_0)$. In the complex case, set $g(x)=f(x)-i\,f(ix)$ (complex-linear, $\Re g=f$); in the real case, $g=f$. Then $\Re g(x)<\Re g(x_0)$ for all $x\in E$. $\square$

Applying the Lemma with $E=C$ and $x_0=0$ yields a **nonzero** continuous linear functional $g$ with
$$\Re g\bigl((b+u)-a\bigr)<0,\qquad\forall\, b\in B,\;u\in U,\;a\in A,$$
that is,
$$\Re g(b+u)<\Re g(a),\qquad\forall\, b\in B,\;u\in U,\;a\in A. \tag{2}$$

## Step 3 — Upgrading to a strict uniform gap

Since $A$ is compact and $\Re g$ is continuous, the infimum is attained:
$$\alpha:=\inf_{a\in A}\Re g(a)=\min_{a\in A}\Re g(a).$$
From (2), $\Re g(b+u)<\Re g(a)$ for every $a\in A$, so
$$\Re g(b+u)<\alpha,\qquad\forall\, b\in B,\;u\in U. \tag{3}$$

Since $g\neq 0$, the real-linear map $\Re g:X\to\mathbb{R}$ is a nonzero continuous linear functional, hence an open map. As $U$ is open with $0\in U$, the image $\Re g(U)$ is an open subset of $\mathbb{R}$ containing $0$; therefore
$$\varepsilon:=\sup_{u\in U}\Re g(u)>0.$$

For each $b\in B$ and $u\in U$, (3) gives $\Re g(b)+\Re g(u)=\Re g(b+u)<\alpha$. Taking the supremum over $u\in U$:
$$\Re g(b)+\varepsilon\;\le\;\\Re g(b)+\sup_{u\in U}\Re g(u)\;\le\;\\alpha,\qquad\forall\, b\in B.$$

Hence $\Re g(b)\le\alpha-\varepsilon$ for all $b\in B$, and taking the supremum over $b\in B$:
$$\boxed{\sup_{x\in B}\Re g(x)\;\le\;\alpha-\varepsilon\;<\;\alpha\;=\;\inf_{x\in A}\Re g(x).}$$

This is the desired strict separation. $\blacksquare$

